# rule.forbid.inline-decode-friction

## .what
no decode-friction inline in orchestrators. logic that needs mental simulation goes into a named transformer
the test: "do i have to decode this to understand what it produces?" yes → extract; no → inline
decode-friction: positional access `slug.split('.')[0]!`, reduce, filter/map/sort pipelines, compound booleans, regex extracts, date math
not decode-friction: property access, named calls, simple conditionals, `arr.length`, `str.trim()`
heuristic: not named by us or an ehmpathy package? wrap it in a domain-named operation

## .the extract
- `slug.split('.')[0]!` → `asKeyrackKeyOrg({ slug })`
- `items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.amount, 0)` → `computeTotal({ items })`
- `a && b || c && !d` → `isEligibleForDiscount({ order })`
- `await stripe.customers.create(...)` → `sdkStripe.setCustomer(...)`
names follow rule.require.get-set-gen-verbs (as*, is*, get*, compute*)

## .enforcement
decode-friction inline in an orchestrator = blocker

## .see also
rule.require.named-transformers (the require twin) · rule.require.narrative-flow
